WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.799 --> 00:00:03.250
"Nasa tells us today it’s found a new planet."

00:00:03.250 --> 00:00:05.720
-"They have found 7..."
-"7 planets"

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:09.621
"As many as 50..." "...500..." "...3500 planets..."

00:00:09.621 --> 00:00:11.350
"They're pretty sure that they're rocky planets."

00:00:11.350 --> 00:00:13.187
"Every one of them could have liquid water."

00:00:13.187 --> 00:00:15.524
"Scientists calling the discovery quote..."

00:00:15.524 --> 00:00:16.680
"Very exciting stuff."

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:17.526
"Very exciting."

00:00:17.526 --> 00:00:18.825
"I am very excited."

00:00:18.825 --> 00:00:22.435
"I'm excited, just really — this is so fascinating."

00:00:22.435 --> 00:00:24.890
I’m also excited, but I’ve always wondered

00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:29.675
how do people find these little “exoplanets”
from trillions of miles away?

00:00:29.675 --> 00:00:32.258
And how could they possibly figure out 
anything about them?

00:00:32.258 --> 00:00:33.084
Their size?

00:00:33.084 --> 00:00:34.239
Their composition?

00:00:34.239 --> 00:00:36.364
If they'd be a nice place to live?

00:00:36.364 --> 00:00:38.373
Well, here’s how.

00:00:41.535 --> 00:00:45.412
It’s possible to study some things in space
by just taking pictures of them.

00:00:45.412 --> 00:00:47.899
But that works best for really close things

00:00:47.899 --> 00:00:49.846
or really big things.

00:00:49.846 --> 00:00:53.294
Seeing a planet around a star 
is much, much harder.

00:00:53.294 --> 00:00:57.047
The problem with exoplanets 
is they're right next

00:00:57.047 --> 00:00:59.665
to a big, bright, massive star.

00:00:59.665 --> 00:01:01.960
And that star overwhelms the planet.

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:06.455
When we point our most powerful telescopes
at stars that are similar to our sun

00:01:06.455 --> 00:01:09.115
we get images like this.

00:01:09.115 --> 00:01:14.071
These pixels are capturing the glare of starlight
but the star is actually much smaller.

00:01:14.071 --> 00:01:17.014
And any planets would be smaller still.

00:01:17.014 --> 00:01:19.720
For example, if our Sun were the size 
of this ball

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:21.709
the Earth would be this big.

00:01:21.709 --> 00:01:24.897
And if we’re talking diameter, the Earth
is about a hundred times smaller 

00:01:24.897 --> 00:01:26.147
than the Sun.

00:01:26.147 --> 00:01:30.380
But the light reflecting off it is
10 billion times dimmer than the Sun.

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:33.213
So of course it would get lost in the glare.

00:01:33.213 --> 00:01:38.069
It’s like looking for a firefly 
next to stadium floodlights.

00:01:38.069 --> 00:01:42.284
In the ‘70s and ‘80s, astronomers set out 
to find a way around this problem.

00:01:42.284 --> 00:01:46.116
At that time, everyone was excited 
about finding intelligent life.

00:01:46.116 --> 00:01:49.894
We launched two interstellar probes 
that carried messages for aliens.

00:01:49.894 --> 00:01:53.482
Giant radar dishes started listening for voices
from outer space.

00:01:53.482 --> 00:01:57.907
And ET became the highest grossing 
movie of all time.

00:01:57.907 --> 00:02:00.640
But embarrassingly, scientists 
had yet to find

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:04.234
a single planet around another star.

00:02:04.234 --> 00:02:05.800
They had a pretty clear wish list:

00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:08.510
First, just confirm the existence of a planet.

00:02:08.510 --> 00:02:11.306
Second, figure out the size of its orbit.

00:02:11.306 --> 00:02:13.690
We'd really like to know the 
distance from the star

00:02:13.690 --> 00:02:16.324
so we can understand how warm the planet is.

00:02:16.324 --> 00:02:20.091
Is it a boiling hellscape, too close 
to its sun, like Mercury?

00:02:20.091 --> 00:02:23.466
Too distant, like the Jupiter’s 
frozen moon Europa?

00:02:23.466 --> 00:02:27.384
Or just right, the kind of place that 
could have liquid water?

00:02:27.384 --> 00:02:30.170
Third, it would be nice to know 
how dense the planet is.

00:02:30.170 --> 00:02:33.871
Is it a giant planet like Jupiter, made mostly
of hydrogen and helium?

00:02:33.871 --> 00:02:39.287
Or is it one of the prize rocky planets
that's mostly heavier, more dense material?

00:02:39.287 --> 00:02:42.250
And most of all, but hardest to do

00:02:42.250 --> 00:02:48.260
we like to find gases in the atmosphere
that might be associated with life.

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:51.095
Oxygen is a fantastic biosignature gas.

00:02:51.095 --> 00:02:55.894
Our atmosphere is full of it thanks to plants
and photosynthetic bacteria.

00:02:55.894 --> 00:03:00.000
And if there were an intelligent alien civilization
looking back at us

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:02.850
they'll be suspicious that something is here.

00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:07.430
It won't be our city lights,
or the Great Wall of China.

00:03:07.430 --> 00:03:10.340
It will be oxygen, actually.

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:13.740
So the goal was to find a planet,
describe its orbit and its density

00:03:13.740 --> 00:03:18.577
and search for signs of life:
all just by looking at this.

00:03:18.577 --> 00:03:24.806
To detect or study an exoplanet,
we have to work with the star.

00:03:24.806 --> 00:03:28.709
We sometimes think of our solar system
where our Sun is a fixed point

00:03:28.709 --> 00:03:31.834
and that all the planets are 
orbiting the sun.

00:03:31.834 --> 00:03:36.244
But a correction to the picture is our sun
is not exactly fixed.

00:03:36.244 --> 00:03:39.894
The sun pulls on the planets
but the planets pull back.

00:03:39.894 --> 00:03:43.099
The planets and Sun are orbiting, 
what we call

00:03:43.099 --> 00:03:44.920
their common center of mass.

00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:47.540
And that makes the sun wobble back and forth.

00:03:47.540 --> 00:03:50.195
A huge planet will make the star wobble a
lot.

00:03:50.195 --> 00:03:54.186
And a lower mass planet will make the star
wobble much less.

00:03:54.186 --> 00:03:55.860
We just had to wait for technology

00:03:55.860 --> 00:03:59.426
to get sensitive enough
to pick up those wobbles.

00:03:59.426 --> 00:04:04.150
In 1995, Swiss astronomers announced that
a star in the Pegasus constellation

00:04:04.150 --> 00:04:08.476
had an oscillation that repeated 
every 4.23 days.

00:04:08.476 --> 00:04:11.970
With just those data points,
the basics of how gravity works

00:04:11.970 --> 00:04:14.370
and some simple math...

00:04:14.370 --> 00:04:18.419
they calculated that there was a planet
only 4 million miles away from the star.

00:04:18.419 --> 00:04:21.279
That's incredibly close compared to 
our solar system.

00:04:21.279 --> 00:04:24.300
And it was about the size of Jupiter.

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:27.389
Soon the wobble method was turning up 
dozens of planets.

00:04:27.389 --> 00:04:31.664
And, at the same time, revealing 
their distance from their stars.

00:04:31.664 --> 00:04:33.058
But this method couldn’t deliver

00:04:33.058 --> 00:04:37.824
the precise mass or size measurements 
needed to calculate density.

00:04:37.824 --> 00:04:40.909
Luckily, there is another way that we can
indirectly detect planets

00:04:40.909 --> 00:04:43.449
just by staring at their stars.

00:04:43.449 --> 00:04:47.436
So some planetary systems 
are aligned just so.

00:04:47.436 --> 00:04:50.199
Such that the planet goes 
in front of the star,

00:04:50.199 --> 00:04:51.560
as seen from our telescope.

00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:52.889
A so called "transit".

00:04:52.889 --> 00:04:54.224
And that's fantastic.

00:04:54.224 --> 00:04:56.468
Because we can monitor the 
brightness of the star

00:04:56.468 --> 00:04:59.469
and look for a tiny, tiny drop in brightness

00:04:59.469 --> 00:05:02.484
that might mean a planet is going 
in front of the star.

00:05:02.484 --> 00:05:06.826
Our Jupiter's transit signal 
on our Sun is 1%

00:05:06.826 --> 00:05:11.607
Here’s what that 1% drop would look like
in our view of a distant star.

00:05:11.607 --> 00:05:12.919
Pretty hard to see.

00:05:12.919 --> 00:05:18.240
So for our Earth and Sun, it's about 
one part in 10,000.

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:21.749
In 1980s, no one knew how to 
detect a change that small

00:05:21.749 --> 00:05:25.680
and most astronomers concluded that this so
called “transit method”

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:29.283
was “not practical as a 
primary detection technique”.

00:05:29.283 --> 00:05:31.181
It's very rare for a planetary system

00:05:31.181 --> 00:05:34.959
to be so perfectly aligned that 
we can see the transit.

00:05:34.959 --> 00:05:37.990
So we must monitor lots of stars.

00:05:37.990 --> 00:05:42.412
Tens of thousands, or even hundreds of thousands
of stars at a single time.

00:05:42.412 --> 00:05:44.834
And of course, just seeing one drop in brightness
isn’t enough.

00:05:44.834 --> 00:05:48.965
You have to wait around for a second transit
to know how long the orbit takes.

00:05:48.965 --> 00:05:52.539
If you were looking for Earth, that means
you’d have to wait for at least a full year.

00:05:52.539 --> 00:05:53.423
It’s hard.

00:05:53.423 --> 00:05:56.849
It's hard to find long period planets with
the transit method

00:05:56.849 --> 00:05:59.906
because you have to be staring at one place
for a very long time.

00:05:59.906 --> 00:06:03.712
But as before, it was only a matter of time.

00:06:03.712 --> 00:06:07.300
In 2001, a research team was watching 
5 million stars

00:06:07.300 --> 00:06:09.206
in the neighborhood of Sagittarius.

00:06:09.206 --> 00:06:12.770
And the picked up dip in the brightness 
of this one.

00:06:12.770 --> 00:06:16.129
Luckily, they didn’t have to wait around
long for a second transit

00:06:16.129 --> 00:06:22.169
because the planet they found 
shot around its star every 29 hours.

00:06:22.169 --> 00:06:24.270
The telescope they used was on the ground

00:06:24.270 --> 00:06:27.774
which meant it had to peer through
Earth’s turbulent atmosphere.

00:06:27.774 --> 00:06:29.083
Not exactly ideal.

00:06:29.083 --> 00:06:32.159
But the Kepler Space Telescope, 
launched in 2009

00:06:32.159 --> 00:06:34.610
was literally above all that.

00:06:34.610 --> 00:06:40.004
It watched one patch of sky for years
and found thousands of planets.

00:06:40.004 --> 00:06:42.169
Not only was this a good detection technique

00:06:42.169 --> 00:06:45.669
it could also be used to figure out 
the precise mass of the planet. 

00:06:45.669 --> 00:06:47.169
And it’s size too.

00:06:47.169 --> 00:06:50.247
The bigger the planet,
the bigger the drop in brightness.

00:06:50.247 --> 00:06:54.577
And with those elements 
you could finally calculate density.

00:06:54.577 --> 00:06:57.999
So far we've found nearly 5000 planets.

00:06:57.999 --> 00:07:00.740
Hundred are likely rocky worlds like Earth

00:07:00.740 --> 00:07:04.969
and dozens of those seem to be in
the habitable zone of their stars.

00:07:04.969 --> 00:07:07.069
Incredibly, the question has shifted from

00:07:07.069 --> 00:07:08.637
“Do any stars have planets?"

00:07:08.637 --> 00:07:11.490
to “Do any of stars not have planets?”

00:07:11.490 --> 00:07:15.211
But we’re still looking for signs of life.

00:07:15.211 --> 00:07:20.642
How? Well, back in 1999, Sara proposed a way
to investigate the gasses

00:07:20.642 --> 00:07:23.165
in exoplanet atmospheres.

00:07:23.165 --> 00:07:25.468
When that planet goes in front of the star

00:07:25.468 --> 00:07:28.130
some of the star light shines 
through the atmosphere.

00:07:28.130 --> 00:07:32.583
Different gases in that atmosphere
would absorb different wavelengths of light.

00:07:32.583 --> 00:07:36.849
For example, a simple molecule like
hydrogen gas absorbs these bands.

00:07:36.849 --> 00:07:39.922
Other compounds have more complex signatures.

00:07:39.922 --> 00:07:45.123
Sara’s idea was to look for those tiny signals
once that starlight reached Earth.

00:07:45.123 --> 00:07:46.885
But not everyone was on board.

00:07:46.885 --> 00:07:51.590
So one place, I applied for a job, a professor
sat across from me and literally said:

00:07:51.590 --> 00:07:55.550
"I just, I just don't think we're going to
have very many transiting planets."

00:07:55.550 --> 00:07:57.969
They didn't think we'd be able to study atmospheres.

00:07:57.969 --> 00:08:00.385
They didn't think the field was 
going to go anywhere.

00:08:00.385 --> 00:08:04.749
But at least one team ran with the idea
to use the Hubble Space Telescope

00:08:04.749 --> 00:08:08.377
and succeeded in observing 
the first exoplanet atmosphere.

00:08:08.377 --> 00:08:11.349
Unfortunately, it looks like 
this technique works best

00:08:11.349 --> 00:08:14.205
when the star is small, 
and the planet is big.

00:08:14.205 --> 00:08:18.255
Earth-sized planets transiting sun like stars
have such a tiny signal

00:08:18.255 --> 00:08:21.338
and their atmospheres have 
an even tinier signal

00:08:21.338 --> 00:08:23.395
that we may never be able to observe.

00:08:23.395 --> 00:08:25.656
It's like the skin of an onion on an onion.

00:08:25.656 --> 00:08:28.763
We need to move beyond transits 
to a different technique.

00:08:28.763 --> 00:08:30.908
We call this technique direct imaging.

00:08:30.908 --> 00:08:34.611
Okay, I know we started down this whole 
wobble and transit path

00:08:34.611 --> 00:08:38.525
because taking a picture of an exoplanet 
seemed impossible.

00:08:38.525 --> 00:08:40.000
But look at this.

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:43.800
This is a real time lapse of planets 
orbiting a star.

00:08:43.800 --> 00:08:49.440
The trick was to block out the star’s overwhelming
glare with a device called a coronograph.

00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:52.050
Now this view is only possible because 
the planets are

00:08:52.050 --> 00:08:53.430
(a) really huge,

00:08:53.430 --> 00:08:57.114
(b) really far from their star (here’s our
solar system for comparison)

00:08:57.114 --> 00:09:00.946
and (c) they’re all glowing red hot.

00:09:00.946 --> 00:09:04.256
We want to make direct imaging 
better and better.

00:09:04.256 --> 00:09:09.136
So we can move down to smaller and smaller
and cooler planets.

00:09:09.136 --> 00:09:11.910
And to do this we have to go to space.

00:09:11.910 --> 00:09:14.956
To get above the blurring effects 
of Earth's atmosphere.

00:09:14.956 --> 00:09:18.255
And we want to use starshade.

00:09:18.255 --> 00:09:21.726
Starshade sounds like a teen fantasy series.

00:09:21.726 --> 00:09:24.000
(whisper) Starshade.

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:27.243
But it’s actually a proposed 
spacefaring parasol.

00:09:27.243 --> 00:09:29.890
Kind of like a free-floating
coronagraph. 

00:09:29.890 --> 00:09:32.976
Starshade would be about the size 
of a baseball diamond.

00:09:32.976 --> 00:09:35.587
But they tested it out using this model.

00:09:35.587 --> 00:09:37.692
It’s strange flower-like shape

00:09:37.692 --> 00:09:42.287
would stop the starlight from bending around
its edges and overwhelming the telescope.

00:09:42.287 --> 00:09:47.715
And it has to formation fly tens of thousands
of kilometers from a telescope.

00:09:47.715 --> 00:09:50.847
The starshade and telescope will line up perfectly

00:09:50.847 --> 00:09:54.814
like in a very, very straight line together
with the target star.

00:09:54.814 --> 00:09:57.897
This giant space flower would 
have to be launched

00:09:57.897 --> 00:09:59.789
inside a much narrower rocket.

00:09:59.789 --> 00:10:03.531
So NASA has turned to origami for inspiration.

00:10:08.673 --> 00:10:12.921
But it’s not clear when or if Starshade
will get off the ground.

00:10:12.921 --> 00:10:16.211
At the end of 2021, the US astronomical community

00:10:16.211 --> 00:10:18.866
laid out they're priorities in 
an official report.

00:10:18.866 --> 00:10:21.414
"The report went through rigorous peer review

00:10:21.414 --> 00:10:24.477
and represents the consensus view 
of the steering committee."

00:10:24.477 --> 00:10:28.707
These so-called “Decadal Surveys” 
are hugely important.

00:10:28.707 --> 00:10:31.766
In previous decades they’ve advocated 
for Hubble

00:10:31.766 --> 00:10:34.868
and the recently launched 
James Webb Space Telescope.

00:10:34.868 --> 00:10:39.550
"For this decade, our highest priority 
is a telescope

00:10:39.550 --> 00:10:42.420
for observing habitable exoplanets."

00:10:42.420 --> 00:10:46.850
But the report didn’t advocate for a 
Starshade mission this decade.

00:10:58.194 --> 00:11:01.865
Having this attitude that I’m going to explore, 
I’m going to do this anyway

00:11:01.865 --> 00:11:04.155
it’s really key in the field of exoplanets.

00:11:04.155 --> 00:11:06.566
So, about a decade later I went back

00:11:06.566 --> 00:11:10.000
and somehow on my schedule was this same professor.

00:11:10.000 --> 00:11:12.829
And he just welcomed me into his office 
and he just said

00:11:12.829 --> 00:11:17.250
“Exoplanets! I always knew it was gonna be big.”

00:11:17.250 --> 00:11:19.651
It was just like: yeah.

